SUBJECT: follow-up to pull request #295 (merge commit c8cf5f9, "fix(mcp): refuse calls to a provider disabled in config"). That PR made every tool refuse a provider with providers.<name>.enabled: false. It left one gap: when the config file exists but cannot be read, the grok and gemini bridges and the unified server treat every provider as ENABLED, while the openrouter bridge refuses. So a user who disabled a provider and then breaks the JSON gets that provider called again.

PLAN (revision v2; changes from v1 are marked [v2])

Goal: when ~/.config/deliberation/config.json exists but cannot be read or parsed, every provider call fails closed with errorKind "config" and a message naming the file and the error, instead of falling back to defaults. A missing config file keeps today's defaults.

1. Shared message in core/provider.js: configErrorMessage(error, configPath) returns "Deliberation config at <path> could not be read (<error>). Fix the file or move it aside; with no file, the defaults apply." "Cannot be read" = the reader returns ok:false: JSON parse failure, and any read error other than ENOENT (EACCES, EISDIR). ENOENT stays ok:true with defaults (unchanged). [v2] This is a message helper only; each surface wraps it in its own existing envelope (step 3), never returns a bare object.

2. [v2] Reader cache key (server/openrouter/config.js makeConfigReader): re-read when any of mtimeMs, ctimeMs, size or ino changes, not mtime alone. chmod changes ctime but not mtime, so today a cached good read hides a newly unreadable file and a cached EACCES survives the repair. A stat failure other than ENOENT (e.g. the parent directory loses search permission) is itself an ok:false result, not a cache hit.

3. [v2] Where the guard sits and what each surface returns. The guard reads the raw reader result (ok:false), never a derived block: grokConfigBlock(), geminiConfig() and getConfig() keep their {} fallbacks for model/timeout pins, but they are no longer consulted for the "may I call a provider" decision.
   a. Unified server, ask-gpt/ask-gemini/ask-grok/ask-openrouter and ask-one: same envelope as the disabled refusal today: content text JSON { result: { provider, isError: true, errorKind: "config", retryable: false, ms: 0, message } }.
   b. Unified server, ask-all and the eight expert tools: their normal envelope with zero provider results, plus top-level error (the shared message) and errorKind "config"; MCP isError true. No provider is called.
   c. Unified server, consensus (server-arbiter): terminal { status: "unresolved", stopReason: "config-error", error: message } - the same shape as the existing stopReasons (no-providers, budget-exhausted). Today it only pushes a "config not loaded" warning and dispatches anyway.
   d. Unified server, consensus-step: init, record_blind, submit_adjudication and submit_revision are local transitions and keep working. dispatch_peers returns { error: "config", errorKind: "config", message, status: "await_peers" } and leaves the session state untouched, so the user fixes the file and calls dispatch_peers again in the same session. It is NOT terminal.
   e. In addition to the tool-level checks, one backstop in the unified server's shared provider call path (the wrapper every provider call goes through, where runContext is set): if the reader is ok:false, the call returns the config error without reaching the adapter. A new tool that forgets the tool-level check still fails closed.
   f. Grok and Gemini bridges: at the top of every tools/call branch that reaches xAI or agy (grok, grok-reply, gemini, gemini-reply), read the reader result first; on ok:false send { content: [{ type: "text", text: "Error: " + message }], isError: true, errorKind: "config", retryable: false } and logCall(id, name, "config", ms). The Gemini bridge gains the logCall it lacks today. Tools with no provider request (grok file listing/cleanup) keep working.
   g. OpenRouter bridge: already fails closed; switch the text to the shared message. openrouter-list keeps its JSON payload shape ({ delegates: [], selected: [] ...}) with error = the shared message and a new errorKind: "config" field.

4. [v2] Server started on a broken file ("late start"). The unified server reads providers.<name>.model, reasoningEffort and timeout once at startup (makeRuntime constructor args) and clones startupConfig once. If that first read was ok:false, those snapshots are empty and would stay empty after the user fixes the file, so calls would silently run on built-in pins until a restart. Fix: extract the provider construction in makeRuntime into buildProviders(providersConfig). When the startup read failed, the server records startupFailed = true; on the first later ok:true read it rebuilds the providers from that read, swaps them into the registry, and re-clones startupConfig from it, once. After that, the normal rule applies (pins need a restart; enabled is live). A start with a good file is unchanged.

5. Hot reload: with steps 2 and 4, fixing the file (content or permissions) clears the error on the next call with no restart, including for a server that started on the broken file.

6. Tests (new file test/config-fail-closed.test.js). Per surface - unified ask-* and ask-one, ask-all, one expert tool, consensus, consensus-step dispatch_peers, grok bridge, gemini bridge, openrouter bridge (+ openrouter-list):
   (a) bad JSON -> errorKind config, the message names the path and the parse error, the provider stub is never called;
   (b) fix the file in place -> the next call succeeds (hot reload);
   (c) missing file -> defaults, calls go through;
   (d) EACCES via chmod 000 on a previously good file with a cached read, then chmod 644 -> errorKind config, then success (skipped on Windows and as root).
   Plus: [v2] (e) broken at process start, fixed in place: the next ask uses the file's model and timeout and still honors enabled:false; (f) consensus-step: dispatch_peers on a broken file returns the error, the session stays in await_peers, a second dispatch_peers after the fix proceeds to await_adjudication; (g) the call-path backstop refuses when the tool-level check is bypassed (call the wrapper directly).

7. Docs: TECHNICAL.md (config load states, the new stopReason), SETUP.md (what a typo does and how to recover), docs/tool-guide.md (errorKind config), README config summary.

Out of scope: schema-validation warnings that do not make the reader fail (unknown keys, bad values the validator replaces with defaults) keep today's warn-and-continue behavior. Pins changing on an ordinary (good -> good) edit still need a restart, as today.

